{
  "_": "The world's components and the models that draw them, one place (realistic.gd reads it through data.gd; docs/TUNING.md). A placer asks for a component (fence, house_poor, keep...), never a file, so a new art family is an edit here. Shape: Paradox's entity files and RimWorld's ThingDef graphicData. Per component: kind (what the town rules ask for: house, hall, barn, prop, clutter...) and variants. Per variant: model (under res://assets; a folder means its scene.gltf), name (the name placers used before the catalogue, kept while they move over), length (its true longest side in metres: the scale), height (its true height in metres, used for the scale instead when the length misleads: a tall stack, a lying cluster), and optionally strip (parts the land supplies itself), floorCut (game units: triangles under it dropped), stone (no glow), dye (materials by name, each colour multiplied by [r, g, b] and renamed Dyed_<name> so team colour leaves it: a market stall's awning; or {\"paint\": [r, g, b]}, the cloth repainted in that colour with its weave kept: the yard washing's linen), weight (how often it's picked). Order matters: it is the order placers pick in. heightRange: the true height a component should stand, in metres (lowest, highest): tools/scale_check.gd fails a model outside it (a hut must never stand as tall as a castle tower). builder: a variant our builders raise from the Quaternius Medieval Village kit (builder house: house_builder.gd, spec w and d in 2 m cells, floors, style plaster|timber|brick, seed, chimney (the chance of one, 0.7 unset); builder castle: castle_builder.gd, spec w, d bailey cells, wall_storeys, keep cells, keep_storeys, tower_storeys, side_towers, banner, seed), built in metres. The castle list is by rank: the crown's seat takes the first, a great house's the last.",
  "matte": ["MI_RockTrim"],
  "woods": {
    "_": "Which trees the woods grow (forest.gd). Quaternius Stylized Nature, the painted look. kit: per role (pine, oak, bush) the model files and the true height in metres a grown one stands; young and sapling: how tall the younger trees are, as a share of grown. Grown trees stand one to two houses tall (a kit cottage about 6 m, a townhouse about 9 m: tools/scale_check.gd holds the grown heights to tree_range, metres); they were 17 and 15 m and read huge beside the houses from mid zoom (Warcraft III: clustered stylised trees at a house and a half). pack_scale: the realistic packs come at their true size (13-31 m); this share of it.",
    
    
    "tree_range": [8, 14],
    "kit": {
      "shade": { "_": "forest.gd shade_at: each tree's leaves a little lighter or darker (light) and warmer or cooler (warm) than its kind's, from where it stands (Warcraft III tree lines: no two crowns one green).", "light": 0.14, "warm": 0.08 },
